    "Step 4 — Remember the reward of restraint. The Qur'an praises those who restrain their anger and pardon people (Surah Aal-e-Imran, verse 134, in meaning). Many scholars note that holding back anger for Allah's sake is one of the hardest — and most rewarded — acts of worship, precisely because it protects families from words that cannot be taken back.",
    "Step 5 — Repair quickly. If anger still slips out, apologise the same day. A fast, sincere apology heals small cracks before they become walls.",
    "A gentle note on differences: scholars across schools agree on these core steps; they differ only on small details of wording and practice, and all of them agree that controlling anger is a sign of strength, not weakness.",
